<h3>Obsidian Web Clipper</h3>
<p>Clip web articles with the Chrome extension <strong>Obsidian Web Clipper</strong> and put them into the vault's <strong>inbox</strong>. The instructor has placed the capture rules (a JSON template) in the wiki vault.</p>
<ul>
    <li>Register your vault names under <strong>vault storage</strong> in Settings → General options, and you can specify which vault to send to.</li>
    <li>Import the template (JSON) and move the <strong>Web Article</strong> template to the very top (it fires first). There are also templates triggered by address, such as <code>x.com</code> or <code>youtube</code>.</li>
    <li>When you capture, it is saved to a folder you designated in advance, such as <code>0.2 inbox/article</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The inbox skill — collect, then analyze</h3>
<pre><code>/inbox        <span class="c"># analyzes the material collected in the inbox and proposes topic-wise bundles</span></code></pre>
<p>Collect several items in advance and run <code>/inbox</code>, and it proposes them <strong>split up by topic</strong>, like "3 of the 5 have the same grain, shall we ingest them together?"</p>

<h3>Token tip — scrape the original</h3>
<p>If you only give a link when ingesting, the AI has to <strong>scrape</strong> the original before it can start, which costs more tokens. <strong>Scraping in advance with the Web Clipper</strong> to secure the original before you start saves tokens.</p>

<h3>Query in practice</h3>
<pre><code>/query Summarize the academic value of the LLM wiki vault.
I am going to cite the bibliographic information with a reference manager later.</code></pre>
<p>If the wiki vault is not enough, it is set up to search all the way into the <strong>main vault</strong> and bring things back, and how I asked the question is recorded along with it. The result includes not just the answer but even recommendations on <strong>how best to design Obsidian metadata (author, created, source, category, and so on)</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Do not put a whole paper in</span>
    Rather than ingesting the entire full text of a paper, it is better to let it learn just the basic concepts from something like the <strong>abstract</strong> and to leave behind <strong>the location of the full text</strong>. Have it read only the important key papers in full, and it is more useful if you put your own commentary and experimental context in alongside.
</div>
